            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\": \"Believers living as temporary residents or pilgrims in the earthly world, signifying that their ultimate citizenship, allegiance, and home belong to the Kingdom of Heaven.\", \"primary_basis\": [\"Hebrews 11:13\", \"1 Peter 1:1\", \"1 Peter 2:11\", \"Philippians 3:20\"], \"alternatives\": [{\"meaning\": \"The vulnerable, marginalized person, or Christ himself in disguise, to whom believers are commanded to show hospitality and love.\", \"basis\": [\"Matthew 25:35\", \"Hebrews 13:2\", \"Deuteronomy 10:19\"], \"prominence\": \"dominant\"}, {\"meaning\": \"Gentiles or unbelievers who were formerly alienated from God's covenants of promise, but are now brought near through Christ.\", \"basis\": [\"Ephesians 2:12\", \"Ephesians 2:19\"], \"prominence\": \"common\"}], \"has_settled_consensus\": true, \"confidence\": \"high\", \"notes\": \"The symbolism of the stranger or sojourner is well-established in two main streams of Christian thought: the believer's existential alienation from the fallen world (pilgrim theology) and the ethical mandate of hospitality where the stranger represents the presence of Christ.\"}",
